{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Do you know Laban the son of Nahor?</b> He should have said 'Laban the son of Bethuel,' and instead of 'we know' (יָדַעְנוּ) he should have said 'we know him' (יְדַעֲנוּהוּ). And it appears that he did not ask them whether they were acquainted with him or not, for he took it as obvious that the people of his own place know him; rather, he asked them about his deeds and about his lineage. For about his deeds he asked whether he traces himself after Nahor, who was a whole and upright man, or after his father Bethuel the deceiver, who violated every virgin; and he said, 'Do you know Laban' — whether he is 'the son of Nahor,' resembling him in character; and they said, 'We know' (יָדַעְנוּ) — that he is the son of Nahor, tracing himself after him and resembling him in his deeds. And about his lineage he asked whether he was born from Nahor the brother of Abraham, for perhaps his mother bore him from another man, and he wanted to know whether no evil report had gone out about his mother, as had gone out about Bethuel who violated every virgin, and he feared that perhaps his wife too had committed adultery so that Laban was born from another man and does not trace himself after Nahor; and they answered, 'We know' (יָדַעְנוּ) — that he is surely the son of Nahor, for no blemish whatsoever was heard about his mother. And he said to them, 'Is it well with him?' — for he knew that all the people of his place were wicked, and if he is righteous according to their words, then surely there is no peace for him because of the wicked, whose way it is to contend with the righteous; and they said, 'Peace,' and behold Rachel his daughter is coming with the flock — and had he no peace, he would not send his daughter among the shepherds lest they molest her." "And there are those who say that this is why he said to them, 'Behold, the day is still great' (Genesis 29:7) — to test them whether they were suspect regarding theft, for shepherds in general are suspect (see Rashi's commentary), and Jacob reasoned that if they are not upright, then Rachel's being alone with them was not according to law, for the license to be secluded applies only in a city with two men, and in the field with three upright men. Therefore the verse specified for you that there were three flocks of sheep, and they were upright — and if so, it was according to law that she was secluded with them."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:06.761612110Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}